  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 205 AM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... All of central North Carolina will be under a \nslight (level 2 of 5) risk for severe thunderstorms today, primarily \nin the late afternoon and evening hours.\n\nA cluster of showers and thunderstorms is currently near the \nMichigan/Ohio border and is forecast to drop to the south-southeast \novernight, reaching West Virginia by morning and continuing to the \nsoutheast. The cluster is likely to be dissipating as it reaches \nNorth Carolina, bringing some scattered showers to the western half \nof the state, but the thunderstorm potential is minimal with this \nwave.\n\nHowever, as a cold front moves to the south behind the initial \ncluster of showers, this will provide more widespread forcing for \nprecipitation and is likely to bring showers to much of central \nNorth Carolina, with the highest chances for rain to the east of US-\n1. It is with this second round of precipitation that severe weather \nwill be potentially locally. The reason for noting the initial \ncluster of showers is that the remnant cloud cover is likely to \nreduce the amount of instability farther to the west, reducing, but \nnot eliminating, the chance for severe weather in the Triad. Most of \nthe area will have MUCAPE values at least 2000 J/kg, and the \neffective shear will be 30 to 40 kts, with the greatest shear to the \nnorthwest. The most likely timing for severe weather will be from 4-\n8pm, moving from northwest to southeast. The 06Z forecast from the \nStorm Prediction Center is very similar to their forecast from \nMonday afternoon: the primary threat will come from damaging wind \ngusts, there will be a secondary threat for isolated tornadoes, \ngreatest from the Triangle north and east, and isolated large hail \ncannot be ruled out to the west of US-1. All rain should come to an \nend shortly after midnight.",
